= Governance =

BITwiki governance exists to protect knowledge quality, provenance, revisionability, and human agency without turning editorial policy into a second ontology.

== Editorial principles ==
* Distinguish fact, interpretation, hypothesis, model, opinion, and metaphor where the distinction matters.
* Prefer source-backed claims and preserve citations when reorganizing content.
* Preserve materially different viewpoints when they are relevant and attributable.
* Make uncertainty explicit rather than manufacturing certainty.
* Correct errors through revision; do not preserve mistakes merely because they are old.
* Preserve provenance when merging, splitting, moving, or rewriting pages.

== Rational objectivity ==
BITwiki aims for evidence-grounded, logically coherent knowledge. It does not claim a perfectly neutral or perspective-free view. Bias and perspective should be made inspectable rather than hidden.

Speculative, philosophical, metaphysical, and exploratory material may exist when clearly framed and not presented as established empirical fact.

== Human and machine collaboration ==
Human and AI contributions should be reviewable, attributable where appropriate, and subject to correction. Automation may assist research, structuring, citation, navigation, and maintenance but does not replace epistemic responsibility.

== Revision and arbitration ==
Disagreement should normally produce better sourcing, clearer distinctions, stronger definitions, or explicit competing interpretations. Governance mechanisms should resolve structural/editorial conflicts without freezing legitimate inquiry.

== Public and protected content ==
Public Git mirrors public wiki content only. Protected content and identity-bound memory require separate permission boundaries and are outside this public repository.

